	Build order

Twelve steps. Each one ends with something that runs and something that is
tested. A step that leaves the tree red is not finished.

	step 1	RV64I core			done
	step 2	UART, "Hello, RVM!"		done
	step 3	CSRs and traps			done
	step 4	MMU: Sv39, Sv48, Sv57		done
	step 5	CLINT				done
	step 6	PLIC				done
	step 7	virtio-blk			done; chains were truncated at 16 descs, fixed
	step 8	boot Debian			verified: /init hands off to sysvinit on the disk
	step 9	virtio-net			verified in-guest: DNS, TCP, and a 12.9 MB fetch
	step 10	virtio-gpu, virtio-input	not started
	step 11	virtio-snd			not started
	step 12	hot block JIT			not started


Rules
-----

These are constraints, not preferences. A change that breaks one needs a
decision recorded here first.

C11, libc and libm. No glib, no zlib, no vendored third party code. The desktop
binary is under 300 KB and CI asserts it -- it sat at 340 KB for a while with
nobody noticing, all of it debug info, so a release build carries no -g and
MODE=debug is one make away when something needs a line number. The APK stays
under 5 MB and CI fails the build if it does not.

On Android: no AndroidX, no Material, no Compose, no WebView. One Activity,
three custom Views, a LinearLayout of buttons. Terminal on Canvas, graphics on
a SurfaceView, audio on AAudio. The dependencies block stays empty.

One thread. No locks in src/, no allocations on the hot path. Memory, the TLB
and the virtio rings are allocated once in vm_new(). The only shared state in
the whole project is the Android input ring and vm.running, which is atomic.

No DMA engine. Device transfers are memcpy between the guest descriptor chain
and a host buffer.

No clever JIT. When step 12 lands it translates hot basic blocks, keys the
cache on guest PC, falls back to the interpreter for everything else, and does
no register allocation and no optimisation passes.

The console itself is black with light glyphs; the paper palette is for the
chrome around it. (DECISION: a #F5F4F0 terminal on a phone reads as a misprint,
and every row a button row costs an 80-column grid is a row of guest output. The
helper keys therefore live with the keyboard, not on the screen, and the actions
that used to be a toolbar are in the header menu.)

Soft colours and no decoration in the UI. #F5F4F0 paper, #2B2B28 ink, #D8D5CE
rules. Monospace in the terminal. No animation, no elevation, no gradients.
Icons are single weight line vectors.


Step 1: RV64I core
------------------

src/cpu/cpu.c, src/cpu/compressed.c, src/cpu/fp.c.

RV64I plus M, A, F, D and C. Compressed instructions are expanded to their
32-bit form before decode so there is one execution path and c.addw cannot
drift away from addw.

Tested by tests/test_cpu.c. The bugs that were found and are now covered,
because everyone falls into them:

LUI sign extends its immediate. th_li() in the harness zero extends to 32 bits.

The B and J immediates are bit permuted, not contiguous. An off by one in the
reassembly corrupts every branch in the system.

funct6, not funct7, discriminates the RV64 shift immediates.

INT64_MIN / -1 has to be handled before the host division runs or the host
raises SIGFPE.

mulhsu zero extends rs2. amomin and amomax compare signed, amominu and amomaxu
compare zero extended, and the value written back to the register is always the
old memory value.

fcvt to integer saturates out of range and raises only NV. Earlier inexactness
still accumulates in fflags.

Anything that returns from cpu_exec32() before the shared epilogue has to
advance c->pc itself, and anything that writes c->pc, meaning mret and sret,
has to have its result copied into npc.

Signed overflow is undefined in C even where RISC-V specifies a wrap. addw,
subw, addiw and mul are computed in the matching unsigned type.


Step 2: UART
------------

src/devices/uart.c. ns16550a at 0x10000000, IRQ 10, 16 byte FIFO, DLAB
divisors. The transmit sink is a function pointer so tests capture output
instead of printing it.


Step 3: CSRs and traps
----------------------

src/cpu/csr.c and the SYSTEM opcode in cpu.c. Machine and supervisor CSRs, the
trap path with medeleg and mideleg delegation, mret, sret, wfi, sfence.vma,
ecall and ebreak. src/vm/sbi.c has the SBI a kernel needs: base, timer, console
putchar, system reset.

mstatus.SD is computed on read from FS and XS. It is architecturally read only
and a stored copy goes stale the moment an FP instruction retires.


Step 4: MMU
-----------

src/mmu/mmu.c. Sv39, Sv48 and Sv57 in one loop parameterised by level count.
Superpages at every level including the misaligned PPN check. A and D are
managed by hardware, which the spec permits and which avoids the trap storm a
software managed implementation causes on Linux. Direct mapped TLB with a
generation tag, so sfence.vma invalidates by bumping the tag.

R=0,W=1 is reserved and faults. W=1,X=1 is legal. Treating the second as
reserved faults on every writable and executable page Linux maps.


Step 5: CLINT
-------------

src/devices/clint.c at 0x02000000. msip, mtimecmp per hart, and mtime driven by
clock_gettime at a 10 MHz guest timebase so the guest sees time pass at the
host rate.

clint_tick() advances mtime and returns the MIP bits it drives. Those are two
different values and conflating them made rdtime read as zero.


Step 6: PLIC
------------

src/devices/plic.c at 0x0c000000. Priorities, per context enables, threshold,
claim and complete. best_source() walks the pending bitmaps a word at a time
and skips words with nothing pending and enabled; it runs on every interrupt
refresh and the usual answer is no source.


Step 7: virtio-blk
------------------

src/devices/virtio.c is the mmio transport and the split virtqueue.
src/devices/virtio_blk.c is the device, on slot 0, backed by a host file with
pread and pwrite. Eight mmio slots at 0x10001000 with a 0x1000 stride, IRQs 1
to 8. Empty slots decode as no device present so the guest probe does not
fault.

For whoever writes the next device:

The request header is 16 readable bytes, type, reserved, sector.

Data is readable for T_OUT and carries VRING_WRITE for T_IN. The last
descriptor is one writable status byte. The used ring len counts all device
written bytes, status included.

Reusing a descriptor chain across a direction change needs put_desc re-issued
with the flipped VRING_WRITE flag.


Step 8: boot Debian
-------------------

Verified along the path a phone actually takes: the released Image, the busybox
initramfs and the Debian disk boot, virtio-blk appears as /dev/vda with the
right size, and /init hands the console over to the init on the disk. Pass
rvm.shell=1 to stay in the initramfs instead, which is the debugging mode. The
tools/mkrootfs.sh debootstrap route is still unverified: it wants root, a couple
of gigabytes and a network, none of which the development host had. Building a rootfs needs root, a couple of gigabytes of
RAM and a route to deb.debian.org, and none of that existed where this was
developed. What ships is the tooling plus the parts of step 8 that are
unit tested, which is the FDT builder and the ELF loader.

tools/mkrootfs.sh runs debootstrap --arch=riscv64 --variant=minbase trixie
under a qemu-user-static chroot with --second-stage, swaps systemd for
sysvinit-core, sets a root password, writes an inittab with a ttyS0 getty and
an fstab for /dev/vda, /proc and /sys, then packs the tree into an ext4 image
with mke2fs -d.

tools/fetch_kernel.sh pulls linux-image-6.12+deb13-riscv64 from the Debian pool
and extracts the uncompressed ELF vmlinux. The loader wants real ELF64 with
PT_LOAD segments, not a compressed Image and not an EFI zboot stub.

dtb/rvm.dts is compatible = "rvm,virt", memory@80000000, cpus with
riscv,isa = "rv64imafdc" and mmu-type = "riscv,sv57", serial@10000000,
clint@2000000, plic@c000000 and the eight virtio_mmio nodes.

Success is this on the console:

	Debian GNU/Linux 13 rvm ttyS0

	rvm login:

Two dts rules learned the hard way. Give interrupt controllers real labels, so
plic: plic@c000000, because dtc rejects a &plic reference to a node it cannot
label. And never write phandle = <N> by hand; let dtc allocate them.


Step 9: networking
------------------

src/devices/virtio_net.c on slot 1. Receive and transmit queues, a TAP backend
on Linux, and a user mode fallback for Android, which has no TAP device.

The user mode stack stays small. ARP for the gateway, a DHCP offer of
10.0.2.15/24, enough IP to route, and just enough TCP and UDP to carry a
connection through NAT. No fragment reassembly, no IPv6.

Nothing in it may block, because the poll runs inside the VM loop: a blocking
read there is the guest stopping. The resolver forward used to wait up to two
seconds on 8.8.8.8, longer than busybox will wait for an answer, so every lookup
was late and lost at once; it forwards, returns, and a later tick collects the
reply. The same rule is why both connection tables have idle timers: without
them the sixteen UDP slots fill up on the seventeenth lookup and the network is
dead for the rest of the boot. Both are visible in --stats, which counts rx and
tx packets.

On the host, for the TAP path:

	ip tuntap add dev rvm0 mode tap
	ip link set rvm0 up
	ip addr add 10.0.2.1/24 dev rvm0
	echo 1 > /proc/sys/net/ipv4/ip_forward
	iptables -t nat -A POSTROUTING -s 10.0.2.0/24 -j MASQUERADE

The relay also has to behave like a sender, not a pipe.  It used to take
whatever recv() returned, hand it down and forget it; if the guest had no RX
descriptor posted the frame was dropped, and if the queue in front of the device
was full the frame was never queued at all.  Nothing re-sent either one, so a
transfer died a few dozen frames in -- a resolver answer and a 22 KB Release
file were fine, which is how this survived every test until apt.  So the relay
reads the guest's acknowledgement out of every segment it receives, stops reading
the socket once 65280 bytes are unacknowledged (the ceiling without window
scaling, and the guest's own scaling request is not honoured because the
handshake never offers it), keeps that much unacked data so a hole can be filled
by replaying from the first byte the guest is missing, and holds a refused frame
at the head of its queue instead of losing it.  Patience is deliberate: a guest on
a phone is slow, not broken, so replays keep coming and the idle timer decides
when a connection is over.  tests/test_net.c drives 120 KB through it with a fake
guest that refuses frames and loses segments.

tools/run.sh --net does all of it. Acceptance is that apt update, curl, wget,
ssh out and ping work inside the guest.

Status as of 0.3.11: ping, DNS, curl (138 612 B and 12 910 137 B twice) and
apt's install/dpkg paths work in the shipped image.  `apt-get update` does not:
it aborts with `std::bad_alloc` inside apt a few seconds in, before the first
`Get:` line, i.e. before it asks the relay for anything, so the *network* half of
this acceptance is met and what remains is apt's own arithmetic (the `-1` that a
phone saw printed as `Reading package lists... 4294967295%`).  Every host-side
explanation was tested and cleared in the same boot - RAM size, swap,
`CommitLimit`, `overcommit_memory`, `ulimit -v`, the stale 45 MB `pkgcache.bin`
and 90 MB of baked-in indexes, `Acquire::Languages=none`, the three
`APT::Cache-*` sizes, `/etc/apt/apt.conf.d`, and `lists` on tmpfs; details in
CHANGELOG.  Next step is an strace of apt, which needs `strace` in the image - a
debug-only run of rootfs.yml, not a change the phone has to download.


Step 10: graphics
-----------------

src/devices/virtio_gpu.c on slot 2, src/devices/virtio_input.c on slot 3. The
GPU implements resource_create_2d, resource_attach_backing,
transfer_to_host_2d, set_scanout and resource_flush into a host framebuffer.
Input reports EV_REL and EV_KEY from the Android touch layer.

The framebuffer reaches GfxView, which is the SurfaceView that already exists,
through the onFrame(byte[] rgba, int w, int h, int stride) callback that
rvm_jni.c already declares. Acceptance is X11 or Wayland coming up inside the
guest with a visible window and a working pointer.


Step 11: audio
--------------

src/devices/virtio_snd.c on slot 4. PCM only. One queue, one stream, no mixer,
no volume control. The host end is AAudio on Android and a null sink elsewhere.
android/jni/CMakeLists.txt already links aaudio when the NDK provides it and
defines RVM_HAVE_AAUDIO. Acceptance is aplay working inside the guest and being
audible on the phone.


Step 12: hot block JIT
----------------------

src/cpu/jit.c. Count executions per basic block. After 50, translate the block
and cache it keyed on guest PC. Everything else keeps running in the
interpreter, which stays the reference implementation.

The cache has to be flushed on sfence.vma and on any write to a page that has
been translated, or self modifying code silently executes stale host
instructions. Linux does use self modifying code, in modules and in ftrace.

No register allocation beyond mapping the guest register file to fixed host
locations. No inlining across blocks. Acceptance is 500 MIPS or better on a
sustained integer loop measured with --stats, and a test proving the JIT and
the interpreter agree instruction for instruction across the whole suite.

The interpreter does about 32 MIPS today on a throttled CI runner. Most of what
was slow about it was per instruction syscalls, not decoding; see CHANGELOG.


The pc = 0xfffffffffffffffe bug
-------------------------------

The guest jumps to data. It shows up as a PC of -2, which is what a jalr or a
corrupted return address gives you when it lands two bytes below zero, and it
always means the emulator retired something the guest never intended as code.
Usually a decode bug that got the instruction length wrong, or a trap path that
wrote epc from a stale npc.

The recipe is in docs/DEBUGGING. Narrow the window with -n, arm the trace with
RVM_TRACE_FROM just before the last known good address, read the last retired
instruction, then resolve it with objdump and addr2line. Tracing the whole boot
produces gigabytes and answers nothing.
